# How is a QALY calculated?
prompt: "Where does a weight like 0.7 come from — a questionnaire such as the EQ-5D and a value set from the public? How do time trade-off and standard gamble turn preferences into numbers? How do we get from QALYs over time, with discounting, to incremental QALYs and a cost per QALY?"

## From ticks to a weight
text: {"font_size":30}
sources:
    - id: dolan
      title: Modeling valuations for EuroQol health states
      authors: Dolan P
      year: 1997
      finding: UK EQ-5D-3L value set; worst state (33333) = −0.594

    text eq5d "EQ-5D-5L" x 125 y 652 font_size 32 hidden true
    pieces grid x 420 y 470 width 360 height 320 n 5 rows 5 of grid hidden true
    text rl1 "Mobility" x 125 y 598 hidden true
    text rl2 "Self-care" x 125 y 534 hidden true
    text rl3 "Usual activities" x 125 y 470 hidden true
    text rl4 "Pain" x 125 y 406 hidden true
    text rl5 "Anxiety" x 125 y 342 hidden true
    text h1 "1" x 276 y 652 hidden true
    text h2 "2" x 348 y 652 hidden true
    text h3 "3" x 420 y 652 hidden true
    text h4 "4" x 492 y 652 hidden true
    text h5 "5" x 564 y 652 hidden true
    path t1 #2f6b8f points [[332,598],[343,584],[365,614]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    path t2 #2f6b8f points [[260,534],[271,520],[293,550]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    path t3 #2f6b8f points [[260,470],[271,456],[293,486]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    path t4 #2f6b8f points [[404,406],[415,392],[437,422]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    path t5 #2f6b8f points [[260,342],[271,328],[293,358]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    text code "2 1 1 3 1" below grid gap 30 #2f6b8f font_size 44 hidden true
    label states "1 of 3,125 states" below attach_to code hidden true
    box vs "Value set" x 690 y 460 width 170 height 80 font_size 30 hidden true
    label vs_sub "public survey" below #8f887c attach_to vs hidden true
    arrow a1 from.x 590 from.y 275 to.ref vs hidden true
    path scale points [[900,600],[900,200]] hidden true
    path tk1 points [[885,600],[915,600]] hidden true
    path tk0 points [[885,370],[915,370]] hidden true
    label l1 "1  Full health" left attach_to tk1 hidden true
    label l0 "0  Dead" left attach_to tk0 hidden true
    shape q07 #2f6b8f shape circle x 900 y 531 radius 9 style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    label lq07 "0.7" right #2f6b8f attach_to q07 hidden true
    arrow a2 vs -> q07 hidden true

    card title "From ticks to a weight"

Last time, four years at q = 0.5 became 2 QALYs — but where does a weight like 0.5, or 0.7, actually come from?
    draw scale tk1 tk0 l1 l0

It starts with a short questionnaire, and the most widely used one is the EQ-5D.
    draw eq5d

It asks about five dimensions of health: mobility, self-care, usual activities, pain or discomfort, and anxiety or depression.
    draw rl1 rl2 rl3 rl4 rl5

In the EQ-5D-5L version, each dimension has five levels, from no problems to extreme problems.
    draw grid h1 h2 h3 h4 h5

Our patient on the new treatment ticks one box per row — say, slight problems walking, moderate pain, and none on the rest.
    draw t1 t2 t3 t4 t5

The ticks become a five-digit code, here 2 1 1 3 1: one of 3,125 possible health states.
    draw code states

    camera center.ref code zoom 1.8

    camera reset true

The code alone is not a weight: nobody has yet said how bad moderate pain is compared with trouble walking.
    highlight t1 t4 effect circle

That is the job of a value set: a scoring formula estimated from a survey of the general public.
    draw vs vs_sub

Members of the public value a sample of states, usually with the time trade-off, and a statistical model fills in the rest.
    highlight vs_sub

Feed in 21131, and the value set returns a weight — here, for illustration, 0.7.
    draw a1 a2 q07 lq07

The scale runs below zero too: in the 1997 UK value set for the older three-level version, the worst state scored minus 0.594.
    shape worst #b5482e shape circle x 900 y 233 radius 9 style.fill #b5482e
    label lworst "−0.594" left #b5482e attach_to worst cites ["dolan"]
    text luk "UK 1997, worst" #b5482e x 800 y 195 font_size 22

So a weight joins two things: the patient describes the state, and the public says what it is worth.
    highlight t1 t2 t3 t4 t5 vs

    quiz "The same patient's EQ-5D answers are scored in two countries and get different weights. Why?"
        right: Different value sets: each country's public values the same states differently.
        wrong: The ticks are identical — what else goes into the weight?
        + Different value sets
        * Different questionnaires
        * The patient changed

## Time trade-off
use: qaly_profiles
with:
    x_label: Years
    y_label: Quality of life, q
    x_max: 11
    lines: straight
    profiles:
      - {id: state, label: 'Life A: the state', color: '#2f6b8f', waypoints: [{t: 0, u: 0.7}, {t: 10.1, u: 0.7}], death_at: 10.1}
      - {id: healthy, label: 'Life B: full health', color: '#b5482e', waypoints: [{t: 0, u: 1}, {t: 10, u: 1}], death_at: 10}
    shade_between:
      a: healthy
      b: state
      gain_label: Quality gained
      loss_label: Years given up

    scratch sc work [{"tex":"q = 7 \\div 10 = 0.7"},{"tex":"0.3 \\times 7 = 0.7 \\times 3 = 2.1"}] x 330 y 300 hidden true
    text wtd "Worse than dead: q < 0" #8f887c at.data [3.5,0.15] hidden true
    text y10 "10" at.data [10,-0.06] hidden true

    card title "Time trade-off"

Now that we know the public values the states, how do you ask a person what a state is worth?
    draw axes tick_one tick_zero

The time trade-off offers a choice between two lives.
    draw full_health_line

Life A: ten years in the health state, then death.
    draw curve_state label_state y10

Life B: years in full health, then death — and at first, the same ten years.
    draw curve_healthy label_healthy

With equal years, everyone picks full health.
    highlight curve_healthy

So shorten Life B, a year at a time, until the person can no longer choose between them.
    animate profiles.1.death_at 7 duration 4 ghost ["curve_healthy"]

Say that happens at seven years.
    text y7 "7" #b5482e at.data [7,-0.06]

Then ten years in the state are worth seven healthy years.
    highlight curve_state curve_healthy

    erase curve_healthy_ghost

The weight is the ratio: q = 7 ÷ 10 = 0.7.
    draw sc_box sc_line_1

On the figure, the two lives now enclose equal areas: the quality gained in the first seven years balances the three years given up.
    draw gain_regions label_gain loss_regions label_loss sc_line_2

    erase sc

That is the QALY idea run backwards: indifference means equal QALYs.
    highlight gain_regions loss_regions

For a state some find worse than dead, the question is reframed — a spell in the state followed by full health, against dying now — and the weight comes out below zero.
    draw wtd

So a time trade-off weight is the share of your years you would keep in full health, instead of living them all in the state.
    highlight curve_healthy

    quiz "Someone would give up 2 of 10 years to be free of a state. What is its weight?"
        right: 0.8: they keep 8 healthy years in place of 10 in the state, and 8 ÷ 10 = 0.8.
        wrong: The weight is the share of years kept, not given up.
        * 0.2
        + 0.8
        * 2.0

## Standard gamble
use: decision_tree
with:
    rollback: true
    unit: q
    box:
      x: 40
      'y': 170
      w: 920
      h: 470
    decimals: 2
    root:
      id: choice
      type: decision
      label: Choose
      children: [{label: Gamble, node: {id: gamble, type: chance, label: Treatment, children: [{label: Full health, probability: 1, node: {id: healthy, type: terminal, label: Healthy, payoff: 1}}, {label: Dead, node: {id: dead, type: terminal, label: Dead, payoff: 0}}]}}, {label: Certain, node: {id: state, type: terminal, label: The state, payoff: 0.7}}]

    scratch sc work [{"tex":"0.7 \\times 1 + 0.3 \\times 0 = 0.7"}] x 780 y 290 hidden true

    card title "Standard gamble"

The time trade-off pays in years; the standard gamble pays in risk.
    draw node_choice label_choice

The choice: live on in the health state for certain,
    draw edge_choice_state branchlabel_choice_state node_state label_state

or take a treatment that restores full health with probability p, and kills you with probability 1 − p.
    draw edge_choice_gamble branchlabel_choice_gamble node_gamble label_gamble edge_gamble_healthy branchlabel_gamble_healthy node_healthy label_healthy payoff_healthy edge_gamble_dead branchlabel_gamble_dead node_dead label_dead payoff_dead

If p is 1, the gamble is a free cure, and everyone takes it.
    draw value_gamble best_choice_gamble

    erase best_choice_gamble

    hide prune_choice_state

Lower p step by step, and at some point the person can no longer choose.
    animate root.children.0.node.children.0.probability 0.8 duration 3

Say that point is p = 0.7.
    animate root.children.0.node.children.0.probability 0.7 duration 2

The gamble is then worth 0.7 × 1 + 0.3 × 0, which is 0.7.
    draw sc_box sc_line_1

So the certain state is worth 0.7 too: the weight is read straight off the probability.
    draw payoff_state

    erase sc

Pause and drag p: the gamble's value follows it, and where it matches the certain state is the weight.
    explore

Both methods put the state on the same scale, with full health at 1 and death at 0.
    highlight payoff_healthy payoff_dead

In practice, the same person usually gives a higher weight in the gamble than in the time trade-off — we come back to why.
    point at.ref payoff_state gesture underline

So the standard gamble weighs a state by how much risk of death you would accept to escape it.
    highlight branchlabel_gamble_dead

    quiz "Someone accepts at most a 5% risk of death to be cured of a state. What is its weight?"
        right: 0.95: indifference comes at a 95% chance of full health, so the state is worth 0.95.
        wrong: p is the chance of full health, not of death.
        * 0.05
        + 0.95
        * 0.5

## Over time, to a cost per QALY
vars: {"ts":2,"tn":3.5}
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - '1'
      - '2'
      - '3'
      - '4'
      - '5'
    x_label: Year
    y_label: QALYs in the year
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 0.8
    stage: 0
    box:
      x: 90
      'y': 170
      w: 590
      h: 450
    series:
      - {name: Standard care, values: [[0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0], [0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0], [0.5, 0.483, 0.467, 0.451, 0]]}
      - {name: New treatment, values: [[0, 0, 0, 0, 0], [0.7, 0.7, 0.7, 0.7, 0.7], [0.7, 0.676, 0.653, 0.631, 0.61]]}

    text rs "Standard: {ts:2}" #b5482e x 845 y 560 font_size 30 hidden true
    text rn "New: {tn:2}" #2f6b8f x 845 y 510 font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch scA work [{"tex":"\\frac{q}{(1+r)^{t-1}}"},{"tex":"r = 3.5\\%"}] x 840 y 330 hidden true
    scratch scB work [{"tex":"\\Delta Q = 3.27 - 1.90 = 1.37"},{"tex":"\\Delta C = 30{,}000"},{"tex":"ICER = \\frac{30{,}000}{1.37} \\approx 21{,}900"},{"tex":"21{,}900 < \\lambda = 30{,}000"}] x 830 y 300 font_size 22 hidden true

    card title "Over time, to a cost per QALY"

Now that each year has a weight, we can add up our running example over time.
    draw axes

Standard care: four years at 0.5, one bar per year, for 2.0 QALYs.
    draw bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 bar_4 bar_5 legend rs

The new treatment: five years at 0.7, for 3.5 QALYs.
    animate stage 1 duration 2

    draw rn

But a healthy year far in the future usually counts for less than one today, so future QALYs are discounted.
    highlight bar_5

Each year is divided by (1 + r) to the power of how far away it is; NICE in England uses r = 3.5% for both costs and QALYs.
    draw scA_box scA_line_1 scA_line_2

Watch the bars shrink: year one stays whole, and year five keeps 87% of its weight.
    animate stage 2 ts 1.9 tn 3.27 duration 3

Summed, standard care falls to 1.90 QALYs, and the new treatment to 3.27.
    highlight rs rn

    erase scA

The gain ΔQ shrinks from 1.5 to 1.37 QALYs.
    draw scB_box scB_line_1

The extra cost, ΔC = 30,000, is paid up front here, so it is not discounted.
    draw scB_line_2

The ICER is ΔC ÷ ΔQ: 30,000 ÷ 1.37, about 21,900 per QALY, up from 20,000.
    draw scB_line_3

A payer with a threshold λ of 30,000 per QALY would still say yes.
    draw scB_line_4

So a cost per QALY is a chain: ticks on a form, the public's trade-offs, weights times years, and a discount rate.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 bar_4 bar_5

    erase scB_box scB_line_1 scB_line_2 scB_line_3 scB_line_4

Every link is a choice someone made, and changing any one of them moves the ICER.
    highlight rs rn

    quiz "If the new treatment's extra benefit came 20 years from now instead of within five, what would discounting do to its ICER?"
        right: It raises it more: QALYs twenty years away are divided by a much bigger (1 + r) to the power t, so ΔQ shrinks while the cost paid now does not.
        wrong: A later year is divided by a bigger (1 + r) to the power t.
        + Raise it more
        * Lower it
        * Leave it unchanged

